{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "בקחתו", :translit "b'kachato", :gloss "when he took"} {:he "בקחתו את רבקה", :translit "b'kachato et rivkah", :gloss "when he took Rebecca"} {:he "בקחתו והוא אסור באזיקים", :translit "b'kachato vehu asur b'azikim", :gloss "when he took and he was bound in fetters"} {:he "ויעתר יצחק", :translit "va'yetar yitzchak", :gloss "and Isaac pleaded"} {:he "ויעתר מנוח", :translit "va'yetar manoach", :gloss "and Manoah pleaded"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :masorah, :count 2, :occurrences ["בקחתו את רבקה" "בקחתו והוא אסור באזיקים"]} {:kind :other, :note "Gezera shava linking 'b'kachato' here to Jeremiah's imprisonment and 'va'yetar' Isaac to 'va'yetar' Manoah to derive Isaac's infertility"}], :frame {:text "A gezera shava links the word בקחתו in Genesis 25:20 to בקחתו in Jeremiah 40:1 (בקחתו והוא אסור באזיקים), where it refers to Jeremiah being bound in chains. Just as there 'taking' implies bound, so here Isaac is metaphorically bound — infertile. A second gezera shava links ויעתר יצחק in Genesis 25:21 to ויעתר מנוח in Judges 13:9: just as Manoah's wife was infertile (Judges 13:2), so Isaac was infertile. Isaac's fertility is stated as a divine response to his prayer in Genesis 25:21.", :sources ["Genesis 25:20" "Genesis 25:21" "Jeremiah 40:1" "Judges 13:2" "Judges 13:9"]}} {:quotes [{:he "לבן הארמי", :translit "lavan ha'arami", :gloss "Laban the Aramean"} {:he "הרמאי", :translit "haramai", :gloss "the deceiver"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Letter rearrangement (anagram): the letters of לבן הארמי rearrange to spell הרמאי"}], :frame {:text "The letters of לבן הארמי can be rearranged to spell הרמאי, 'the deceiver,' reflecting Laban's reputation for deception as described in Genesis 31:7, where Jacob states that Laban changed his wages ten times.", :sources ["Genesis 31:7"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :masorah, :count 2, :occurrences ["בקחתו את רבקה" "בקחתו והוא אסור באזיקים"], :by :selah, :located [{:phrase "בקחתו את רבקה", :